CertHub.addPlanDetail("ai-900", {"study":{"1":[["Name the main AI workloads and give one example of each.","Prediction and forecasting (house prices), anomaly detection (card fraud), computer vision (reading licence plates), natural language processing (review sentiment), speech (call transcription), document processing (invoice fields), knowledge mining (searching thousands of contracts) and generative AI (drafting an email reply)."],["How is an AI model different from rule-based software?","Rule-based software follows logic a developer wrote by hand. A machine learning model learns the relationship between inputs and outputs from example data, so it can handle cases nobody wrote a rule for, but its outputs are probabilistic and can be wrong."],["List the six Microsoft responsible AI principles.","Fairness; reliability and safety; privacy and security; inclusiveness; transparency; and accountability."],["Fairness or inclusiveness: what is the difference?","Fairness means the system treats people equally and does not give different outcomes to similar people based on attributes such as gender or ethnicity. Inclusiveness means the system is designed so everyone can use and benefit from it, including people with disabilities or limited connectivity."],["What is the purpose of a transparency note and of Limited Access?","A transparency note explains what a Microsoft AI service can and cannot do, its intended uses and its limitations, supporting transparency. Limited Access means sensitive features, such as face identification, require Microsoft to approve the customer and use case before they can be used, supporting accountability and safety."]],"2":[["What are features and labels?","Features are the input values the model uses (for example size, rooms and location of a house). The label is the value you want to predict (the sale price). Supervised training data has both; unsupervised data has no label."],["Regression, classification or clustering: how do you tell them apart?","Regression predicts a number. Classification predicts a category from known classes (binary for two, multiclass for more). Clustering groups items that have no labels into clusters of similar items."],["Precision vs recall?","Precision is the share of predicted positives that were actually positive (how trustworthy a positive prediction is). Recall is the share of actual positives the model found (how many it missed). A fraud model that must catch almost every fraud needs high recall."],["Why do you hold back validation data?","To measure how well the model performs on data it has not seen. A model that scores well on training data but badly on validation data is overfitting: it memorized the training set instead of learning general patterns."],["What do automated ML and the designer each give you?","Automated ML tries many algorithms and settings for your data and task (classification, regression, forecasting and more) and ranks the resulting models by a metric. The designer is a drag-and-drop canvas for building training pipelines without code."],["What is the difference between a compute instance and a compute cluster?","A compute instance is a single managed VM for a data scientist's notebooks and development. A compute cluster is a group of VMs that scales out for training jobs and can scale to zero nodes when idle."]],"3":[["Classification, object detection or segmentation?","Image classification gives one label for the whole image. Object detection finds each object and returns a label and bounding box for each. Semantic segmentation labels every pixel, giving the exact shape of each object."],["What does OCR return in Azure AI Vision?","The Read feature extracts printed and handwritten text from images and documents, returned as pages, lines and words with bounding box coordinates and confidence values."],["What can Azure AI Face do without Limited Access approval?","Face detection: finding faces and returning their location and attributes such as head pose, glasses, blur or occlusion. Identification and verification (recognizing who a person is) require approval under Microsoft's Limited Access policy, and emotion, gender and age inference were retired."],["When do you use Document Intelligence instead of plain OCR?","When you need structured data from documents, such as fields and values from invoices, receipts, IDs or your own forms, including tables and key-value pairs. OCR alone gives you the text but not its meaning or structure."],["Multi-service resource or single-service resource?","A multi-service Azure AI services resource gives one endpoint and key for several services and one bill. A single-service resource (such as Azure AI Vision) is useful when you want a free F0 tier, separate billing or separate access for that one service."]],"4":[["What does sentiment analysis with opinion mining return?","A positive, neutral or negative label with confidence scores for the whole document and each sentence, and, with opinion mining, the sentiment toward specific aspects such as 'battery' or 'screen'."],["Entity recognition vs entity linking vs PII detection?","Entity recognition finds and categorizes things such as people, places, organizations, dates and quantities. Entity linking disambiguates an entity and links it to a knowledge base entry such as Wikipedia. PII detection finds personal data such as phone numbers and can return redacted text."],["Speech to text vs text to speech?","Speech to text (speech recognition) turns spoken audio into text, for captions or transcripts. Text to speech (speech synthesis) turns text into spoken audio with a chosen voice, for voice assistants and accessibility."],["Conversational language understanding vs question answering?","Conversational language understanding predicts the user's intent (for example BookFlight) and extracts entities (city, date) so an app can take an action. Question answering returns the best matching answer from a knowledge base of question and answer pairs, such as an FAQ."],["What is an embedding?","A vector of numbers that represents the meaning of a token, word or passage. Texts with similar meaning have vectors that are close together, which enables semantic search and similarity comparison."]],"5":[["How does a large language model produce an answer?","It splits the prompt into tokens and repeatedly predicts the most likely next token given everything so far, appending each prediction until it reaches a stop condition or the maximum length. It learned these probabilities during pretraining on large amounts of text."],["What is a system message and why use one?","Instructions sent to the model before the user's input that set its role, tone, rules and output format, for example 'You are a support assistant for Contoso. Answer only from the provided documents.' It shapes every response in the conversation."],["What is RAG and what problem does it solve?","Retrieval augmented generation searches your own data (often with a vector index) for passages relevant to the question and adds them to the prompt so the model answers from them. It grounds answers in current, private information and reduces fabricated answers without retraining the model."],["What does temperature control?","How random the token choice is. Low temperature (near 0) gives focused, repeatable answers suited to facts and extraction; higher values give more varied, creative output."],["Describe Microsoft's four stages for responsible generative AI.","Identify potential harms; measure how often and how badly they occur; mitigate them in layers (model, safety system such as content filters, system message and grounding, user experience); and operate the solution responsibly with a deployment plan, monitoring and incident response."],["What makes something an agent rather than a chat assistant?","An agent combines a model with instructions, knowledge and tools (such as search, code execution or calling an API) so it can reason about a goal and take actions, sometimes over several steps, instead of only replying with text."]]},"domains":[{"id":1,"name":"Describe AI workloads and considerations","w":19,"topics":["What AI is: models that learn patterns from data, and how that differs from rule-based software","Common AI workloads: prediction and forecasting, anomaly detection, computer vision, NLP, document processing and generative AI","Computer vision workloads: image classification, object detection, optical character recognition and facial analysis","Natural language processing and speech workloads: text analysis, translation, speech recognition and conversational AI","Document processing and knowledge mining workloads: extracting fields from forms and making content searchable","Generative AI workloads: creating text, code and images from prompts, copilots and agents","Responsible AI principles: fairness, and reliability and safety","Responsible AI principles: privacy and security, and inclusiveness","Responsible AI principles: transparency and accountability","Responsible AI in practice: identifying harms, human oversight, Limited Access features and transparency notes"],"notes":["Skills measured: Identify features of common AI workloads; Identify guiding principles for responsible AI"],"labs":["No account needed: list ten apps or services you used this week (phone keyboard, photo search, spam filter, navigation, a chatbot) and label each with its AI workload: prediction, anomaly detection, computer vision, NLP, speech, document processing or generative AI. Check your labels against the lesson on common workloads.","No account needed: pick one AI system from your list (for example a loan-approval or hiring model) and write one risk and one mitigation for each of the six responsible AI principles: fairness, reliability and safety, privacy and security, inclusiveness, transparency and accountability.","Free, browser only: read the public transparency note for one Azure AI service (search Microsoft Learn for 'transparency note Azure AI Language' or 'Azure AI Face') and write down its intended uses, its limitations and one feature that needs Limited Access approval. Nothing to deploy, so nothing to delete."]},{"id":2,"name":"Fundamental principles of machine learning on Azure","w":19,"topics":["Features and labels, training and validation data, and how a model is trained and then used for inference","Regression: predicting a numeric value, with evaluation metrics MAE, RMSE and R²","Binary and multiclass classification, with accuracy, precision, recall, F1 and the confusion matrix","Clustering: grouping unlabeled data, and supervised vs unsupervised learning","Deep learning: neural networks, weights and layers, and why they suit images, speech and language","The transformer architecture: tokens, embeddings and attention","Azure Machine Learning: workspace, studio, data assets, compute instances and compute clusters","Automated machine learning (AutoML) and the Azure Machine Learning designer","Deploying models to endpoints for real-time or batch inference, and responsible AI tools in Azure Machine Learning"],"notes":["Skills measured: Identify common machine learning techniques; Describe core machine learning concepts; Describe Azure Machine Learning capabilities"],"labs":["Free, no Azure needed: open a free notebook service (Google Colab or a local Jupyter install) and train a scikit-learn LinearRegression on a small table such as ice-cream sales vs temperature; split the data 70/30 and print MAE, RMSE and R² for the validation set.","Free, no Azure needed: with scikit-learn, train a LogisticRegression classifier on the built-in breast cancer dataset, print the confusion matrix, accuracy, precision and recall, then run KMeans with k=3 on the iris features (without labels) and compare the clusters with the real species.","Free Azure account: create an Azure Machine Learning workspace, open Azure Machine Learning studio and explore the Data, Compute, Automated ML and Designer pages without creating any compute. Cost warning: compute instances and clusters bill while running, and the workspace creates a storage account and Key Vault, so delete the resource group when you finish."]},{"id":3,"name":"Computer vision workloads on Azure","w":19,"topics":["Image classification vs object detection vs semantic segmentation","How computer vision models work: pixels, filters, convolutional neural networks and multimodal models","Azure AI Vision image analysis: captions, dense captions, tags, object detection, people detection and smart crops","Optical character recognition (OCR) with the Azure AI Vision Read feature","Face detection and analysis with Azure AI Face, and the Limited Access policy for identification and verification","Azure AI Document Intelligence: prebuilt models (invoices, receipts, IDs), the layout model and custom models","Creating and using Azure AI services resources: multi-service vs single-service resources, endpoints, keys and the free F0 tier","Choosing the right Azure vision service for a scenario"],"notes":["Skills measured: Identify common types of computer vision solution; Identify Azure tools and services for computer vision tasks"],"labs":["Free Azure account: create an Azure AI Vision resource on the free F0 tier (if offered in your region), open the vision try-it experience in the Foundry portal or Vision Studio, and run image analysis on three of your own photos: compare the caption, tags and detected objects. Delete the resource afterward.","Free Azure account: with the same F0 Vision resource, run OCR (Read) on a photo of a street sign and on a scanned page, and note how lines and words come back with bounding boxes. Stay on the free tier and delete the resource group when finished.","Free Azure account: create an Azure AI Document Intelligence resource on the free F0 tier, open Document Intelligence Studio and run the prebuilt receipt model on a photo of a receipt; compare the extracted fields with the layout model's output. Delete the resource group when you are done so nothing bills after your credit."]},{"id":4,"name":"Natural language processing workloads on Azure","w":19,"topics":["Language detection, sentiment analysis and opinion mining","Key phrase extraction, named entity recognition, entity linking and PII detection","Tokenization, embeddings and semantic similarity: how text becomes numbers","Summarization and custom text classification in Azure AI Language","Speech recognition (speech to text) and speech synthesis (text to speech) with Azure AI Speech","Azure AI Translator for text and documents, and speech translation","Conversational language understanding: utterances, intents and entities","Question answering: building a knowledge base from FAQs for a bot","Choosing the right Azure language or speech service for a scenario"],"notes":["Skills measured: Identify features of common NLP workload scenarios; Identify Azure tools and services for NLP workloads"],"labs":["Free Azure account: create an Azure AI Language resource on the free F0 tier, open Language Studio (or the language playground in the Foundry portal) and run language detection, sentiment analysis with opinion mining, key phrase extraction and PII detection on five product reviews you write. Delete the resource afterward.","Free Azure account: create an Azure AI Speech resource on the free F0 tier and use the speech playground to transcribe a short recording of yourself, then synthesize the transcript with two different neural voices. Stay on F0 and delete the resource group when finished.","Free Azure account: create an Azure AI Translator resource on the free F0 tier and translate a paragraph into three languages in one request from the portal try-it page or with curl; note the detected source language in the response. Delete the resource when done."]},{"id":5,"name":"Generative AI workloads on Azure","w":24,"topics":["How large language models generate text: tokens, next-token prediction and pretraining","Common generative AI scenarios: copilots, chat assistants, content drafting, summarization, code and image generation","Prompt engineering: system messages, user prompts, few-shot examples and clear instructions","Grounding and retrieval augmented generation (RAG) with your own data","Model settings: temperature, top_p and maximum response length","Microsoft Foundry (formerly Azure AI Foundry): hubs and projects, the model catalog and the playgrounds","Azure OpenAI models in Foundry: GPT chat models, embeddings models and image generation models","AI agents: models with instructions, tools and knowledge, and the Foundry Agent Service","Responsible generative AI: identify, measure, mitigate and operate; content filters and Azure AI Content Safety","Evaluating generative AI apps: groundedness, relevance, fluency and safety evaluations, and red teaming"],"notes":["Skills measured: Identify features of generative AI solutions; Identify generative AI services and capabilities in Microsoft Azure; Responsible AI considerations for generative AI"],"labs":["Free, no Azure needed: in any free chat assistant, send the same request three ways: a bare one-line prompt, then with a system-style instruction (role, audience, format), then with two examples of the output you want (few-shot). Compare the answers and write down which change mattered most.","Free, no Azure needed: use a free, rate-limited model playground (for example GitHub Models) to ask a question about a made-up company policy, then paste the policy text into the prompt and ask again. Note how grounding changes the answer, and try temperature 0 vs 1 on the same prompt.","Free Azure account: open the Microsoft Foundry portal, create a project and browse the model catalog, model cards and the content filter settings without deploying a model. Cost warning: model deployments bill per token and there is no free tier for Azure OpenAI, so do not leave deployments running; delete the project's resource group when finished."]}]});
